'#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/49"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "110 metres Hurdles, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "15 – 16 August 2016"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/909"} "Estádio Olímpico João Havelange, Engenho de Dentro, Rio de Janeiro"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/358640"} "40 from 27 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The United States had dominated this event since the 1896 Olympics, but they came to Rio with no dominant hurdler. Their best hurdler in recent years had been " [:a {:href "/athletes/126580"} "Aries Merritt"] ", who set a world record after the 2012 Olympics, and was third at the 2015 World Championships while suffering from end-stage kidney disease. After that meet Merritt underwent a kidney transplant and returned to competition in early 2016, but he failed to make the US team at the trials. Another top hurdler was Cuban " [:a {:href "/athletes/115072"} "Dayron Robles"] ", the 2008 Olympic champion and a former world record holder, but he was not in Rio, out with injuries."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In their absence the event was wide open. The event started in a torrential downpour, which affected the first two heats. The rain then relented, and the officials gave the non-qualifiers from the first two heats a re-run in an extra heat, allowing them to qualify on time, but only " [:a {:href "/athletes/134681"} "Deuce Carter"] " was able to do so. The final was won by Jamaican " [:a {:href "/athletes/134693"} "Omar McLeod"] ", who had won the World Indoor Championships over 60 metres earlier in the year. He was followed by Cuban " [:a {:href "/athletes/124379"} "Orlando Ortega"] " (representing Spain in Rio), with France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/125924"} "Dimitri Bascou"] " taking bronze. " [:a {:href "/athletes/136243"} "Devon Allen"] " finished fifth, the highest placed American. It was the first time in the history of the event that no American won a medal, save for the boycotted 1980 Olympics."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2624"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/134693"} "Omar McLeod"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JAM.png"}] "JAM"]] [:td {} "13.27 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "13.15 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "13.05 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2368"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/124379"} "Orlando Ortega"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ESP"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ESP.png"}] "ESP"]] [:td {} "13.32 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "13.32 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "13.17 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2413"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/125924"} "Dimitri Bascou"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "13.31 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "13.23 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "13.24 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2428"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/133810"} "Pascal Martinot-Lagarde"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] 